{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790289752,"nonce":"radgD6GRjrv0ZBReywJD0JHK2LE=","title":"Lua GC: gaps left after weak tables, __gc and collectgarbage","message":"The L-gc-semantics package gives zylua weak tables (k, v, kv, with ephemeron semantics), __gc finalizers with resurrection and exit-time finalization, collectgarbage options and warn. What it leaves:\n\n- Dead weak keys become tombstones (the key box rewritten as a DEAD_KEY_KIND instance with a nil value) during the collection, because a dict cannot be reindexed while the world is stopped. collectgarbage compacts the tables holding tombstones; a collection the heap budget starts does not, so a weak-key cache that never calls collectgarbage keeps 16 bytes of pair and a 32-byte box per dead key until it does. Compaction at a safe mutator point (the next hash-part insert into a dirty table) would bound it.\n- Finalizers after an automatic collection run inside the allocation that started it, once the collection is over. A finalizer that mutates a structure the interrupted code is in the middle of updating could see it half-updated (probed: appending to the same array part from a finalizer while the program appends to it stays correct). Lua runs them only at its own GC steps.\n- The lowering does not know that any allocation may run a finalizer: a loop like gc.lua's `repeat u = {} until finish`, whose exit depends on a flag a finalizer sets, relies on the flag's upvalue being reloaded each iteration.\n- The stack scan is conservative: an object a stale stack word names is not collected, so a weak entry or a finalizer can outlive the reference Lua drops. gengc.lua:122 (`t[1] = {10}; collectgarbage(\"step\", 0); assert(t[1] == nil)`) stops there.\n- collectgarbage(\"count\") is live bytes after the last collection plus what was spent against the budget since; storage the drop analysis released and the program took again is not counted, so gc.lua:201 (`assert(gcinfo() \u003c x)` after a step) fails.\n- collectgarbage(\"step\") always runs a full collection and returns true; incremental/generational only record the mode; setpause/setstepmul only record their values.\n- A shaped table is never weak; the typer keeps any shape whose metatable may hold __mode (or is unknown) out of slots, and host_gc_note ignores __mode on a shaped table.\n- Only tables, functions and coroutines leave weak tables; a FILE* handle never does.\n\nOfficial files after the package: gc.lua passes the mode checks (line 16) and stops in the load-heavy sections (5000 `load` calls; 63840a4) with its full limits; with those loops shortened and `_soft`, it reaches gc.lua:201. gengc.lua stops at 122 as before (see above). closure.lua passes its weak-table loop and stops at 253 (debug.upvalueid).","files":null}]}